<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Upper Lusatia</h1><p class="st-meta">1158 CE – 1815 CE (approx.) · capital Bautzen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The land of Bautzen and Görlitz, a Sorb homeland ruled for centuries by Bohemia, famed for its Six Towns League and Herrnhut.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, the land of the Slavic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milceni">Milceni</a>, belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-meissen">Margraviate of Meissen</a>. In 1158 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> granted the land of Bautzen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-ii-of-bohemia">Vladislaus II of Bohemia</a>, starting a long Bohemian connection. From the 15th century the region came to be called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a>, in contrast to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> was ruled by distant Bohemian kings but largely governed by its estates and towns. In 1346 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zittau">Zittau</a>, Lauban, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamenz">Kamenz</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a> formed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lusatian-league">Six Towns League</a>, protecting trade routes. The mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakob-bohme">Jakob Böhme</a> lived in Görlitz, and in 1722 Moravian refugees founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrnhut">Herrnhut</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolaus-ludwig-von-zinzendorf">Count Zinzendorf</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1635">Peace of Prague</a> of 1635 transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-lusatia">Upper Lusatia</a> to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony</a>. In 1815 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> gave the north-eastern half, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorlitz">Görlitz</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Today it is split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, with a Sorbian minority around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bautzen">Bautzen</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-upper-lusatia.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Upper Lusatia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: J.-H. Janßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bautzen_Hammermuehle_44.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1158 CE – 1815 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bautzen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vladislaus II of Bohemia, Charles IV</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German, Upper Sorbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oberlausitz, Land of Bautzen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=margraviate-of-upper-lusatia" aria-label="Margraviate of Upper Lusatia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5005 404 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Upper Lusatia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5325 660L5330 692M5325 660L5312 664M5325 660L5312 664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5330 692h0M5312 664h0M5312 664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5325 660h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5325 660h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1486&amp;focus=margraviate-of-upper-lusatia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

